Tenant-visible task failure diagnostics
======================================

FreeSDN task progress keeps its task ID, organization owner, status, timestamps,
progress counts and retry counts. Failed tasks use the stable error code
task_failed and the message "Task failed". Retrying tasks use task_retry and
a retry-count message. Neither record contains the original exception message
or a diagnostic result payload. Revoked records also drop previous diagnostic
errors/results and use "Task was revoked".

The progress store applies this boundary before persistence and when reading
legacy failure/retry/revoked records. Reading does not silently rewrite or delete
older records. Existing records remain in protected Redis until their normal
expiry or an explicitly managed retention operation. Every serving API process
must be updated; old API processes can still expose old diagnostic fields.

Task result queries use the scoped progress store only. They do not fetch the
raw Celery result backend for a task lacking a scoped completed record. That
backend has no trusted organization owner. Missing/unowned tasks and tasks from
another organization keep the existing generic 404 response. No permission is
granted based on possessing a task ID.

The FreeSDN failure/retry hooks and custom task_failure log signal do not render
the exception. Operators retain the task ID for correlation. This change does
not invent a detailed error description from an unknown third-party exception,
and exception objects with an unsafe __str__ cannot break these hooks.

Scope limits
------------

Celery's own result serialization, exception traceback and built-in worker log
remain separate sensitive infrastructure surfaces. The real broker/worker
qualification explicitly demonstrates that those surfaces still contain the
synthetic diagnostic while the corrected tenant progress/API boundary does not.
Protect broker/result storage and logs with appropriate access and retention;
this correction is not a universal secret-removal or log-sanitization guarantee.

Successful task result payloads, explicitly supplied running-task progress
messages, Celery SUCCESS versus domain success, submission recovery, duplicate
execution and post-check authorization races retain their existing contracts.
Task producers must supply appropriate public success/progress fields. Changing
those contracts and sanitizing infrastructure logs require separate work.
